text:
{
  "findings": [
    {
      "ref": "[R173]",
      "area": "content",
      "claim": "A parent reports that the app includes helpful tools that build upon one another to help information make sense and stick with her child who struggles with dyslexia.",
      "stance": "neutral",
      "quote": "My daughter that struggles with dyslexia loves the helpful tools that build upon one another to help the information make sense and stick with her. Thank you Khan Kids"
    },
    {
      "ref": "[R174]",
      "area": "age_gating",
      "claim": "A parent reports that the app is suitable for both fifth graders and two year olds, indicating it works across a wide age range.",
      "stance": "neutral",
      "quote": "Suitable for fifth graders and two year olds"
    },
    {
      "ref": "[R177]",
      "area": "content",
      "claim": "A parent reports that the app helps their child learn about the alphabet, animal sounds, and general knowledge, which they believe will give the child an advantage over peers.",
      "stance": "neutral",
      "quote": "Khan academy kids really helps my child learn about all kinds of cool and interesting stuff like the alphabet and about like animal sounds like really cool stuff like that and I bet when she grows up she’s gonna know a lot more than a lot of other kids did like the kids that do khan Academy kids, so I really think that it’s awesome!"
    },
    {
      "ref": "[R178]",
      "area": "age_gating",
      "claim": "A parent reports that the app is 'for way too little kids' and suggests it lacks content for older children.",
      "stance": "risk",
      "quote": "For way to little kids"
    },
    {
      "ref": "[R180]",
      "area": "content",
      "claim": "A child reports that the math content is 'a little to hard' and requests it be made easier, along with requests for more game variety and features like hats and bags.",
      "stance": "risk",
      "quote": "the math is little to hard can it be a little easier so it can be more fun"
    },
    {
      "ref": "[R182]",
      "area": "content",
      "claim": "A parent reports that the app includes a free library of books and is described as 'the best app I have ever used with my daughter' for early learning.",
      "stance": "neutral",
      "quote": "This has to be the best app I have ever used with my daughter. She is 3 and this app has everything I need to get her started for learning. It includes FREE books! An entire library of books! Such a great resource."
    },
    {
      "ref": "[R183]",
      "area": "age_gating",
      "claim": "A parent reports that the app 'can be for older kids too if they want to' and requests addition of grade levels like fifth grade.",
      "stance": "neutral",
      "quote": "It makes me smart It can be for older kids too if they want to. I’m glad there’s no ads but can you add different grades on it like fifth grade 4th grade 3rd grade? Please 🙏"
    },
    {
      "ref": "[R187]",
      "area": "content",
      "claim": "A parent reports that the app featured a scenario where a child must put a pink bow on a man with a full beard, which they found inappropriate and led to immediate deletion of the app.",
      "stance": "risk",
      "quote": "I liked the games at first and then it played a game where the child has to put a pink bow on a man. A man with a full beard in manly clothes. Put a big pink bow on his head? Not just once but twice. Two men. Really?? That’s the example they chose to use? Deleted immediately."
    },
    {
      "ref": "[R188]",
      "area": "money",
      "claim": "A parent reports that the reward system is 'annoying' and requires long waits, does not help learning, and is 'just stuff for first graders' even when set to 8+.",
      "stance": "risk",
      "quote": "The rewards they are ANNOYING they make me wait sooooooooooo long for it also it really doesn’t help me learn I set it to 8+ but it’s really just stuff for first graders like the sh sound and please make it more age-appropriate ☹️ oh and please make 3rd grade level."
    },
    {
      "ref": "[R193]",
      "area": "content",
      "claim": "A grandparent reports that the app displayed two men with bows and neckties in a scenario that felt inappropriate, prompting them to seek alternatives for teaching reading.",
      "stance": "risk",
      "quote": "I was looking for something for my grandson to get a foundation for reading. During his first time using it, I noticed that there were two men, and who was supposed to put the hats on them. Then he was supposed to put the neck ties on them. Then they would disappear and he was supposed to put the pink bows on their head. Then it would say something like don’t they look nice?"
    },
    {
      "ref": "[R197]",
      "area": "content",
      "claim": "A parent reports that the app is 'very good' and 'perfect for preschoolers and kindergartners' with no ads and free access.",
      "stance": "neutral",
      "quote": "Very good. It’s free, no ads, perfect for preschoolers and kindergartners."
    },
    {
      "ref": "[R202]",
      "area": "content",
      "claim": "A parent reports that their 4-year-old enjoys the app's categories and is learning letters and sounds.",
      "stance": "neutral",
      "quote": "My 4 year old is loving this app!! She enjoys all the different categories and I can tell she’s learning her letters and sounds !!"
    },
    {
      "ref": "[R209]",
      "area": "content",
      "claim": "A parent reports that the app is used as a screen-free learning tool with their 6-year-old who is excelling in math due to the app's repetitive and visual teaching style.",
      "stance": "neutral",
      "quote": "He enjoys all the games he’s played on the app. We are mostly screen free but this doesn’t feel like a video game or “screen time” because we are doing it together and he’s learning. He can do it alone, but he likes doing it with me or his father. He’s 6, by the way."
    },
    {
      "ref": "[R211]",
      "area": "content",
      "claim": "A teacher reports that the app incorrectly suggests practicing letters the child hasn't learned yet (e.g., 'practice letter a' shows 'm'), which they describe as 'frustrating' and 'made poorly'.",
      "stance": "risk",
      "quote": "When you go to practice a certain letter you click on , and it add in a letter they haven’t learned yet is frustrating, and not worth keeping the app. If it says ex: practice letter a why is my child learning m ? As a teacher this was made poorly ."
    },
    {
      "ref": "[R224]",
      "area": "content",
      "claim": "A reviewer states the app 'only teaches uppercase letters' and lacks phonics and letter sound review, which they consider insufficient for learning.",
      "stance": "risk",
      "quote": "App only teaches uppercase letters. Not phonics driven. Does not review letter sounds enough. Does not interact with kids during the stories."
    },
    {
      "ref": "[R230]",
      "area": "content",
      "claim": "A parent reports that the app now includes topics like yoga and books about men in history that they disagree with, and they request the ability to remove such content.",
      "stance": "risk",
      "quote": "My autistic 6 year old loves your app but we can't use it anymore since there is no way to remove content we don't agree with. One of the topics that shows up now that wasn't there last week is yoga and we do not participate in transcendental meditation. Also why aren’t there books available on this app about men who have done things in history."
    },
    {
      "ref": "[R259]",
      "area": "age_gating",
      "claim": "A parent reports that the app is too easy for their autistic 4-year-old who reads at a 1st/2nd grade level and suggests adding a skills test to match curriculum to ability rather than age.",
      "stance": "risk",
      "quote": "My daughter is 4, but she is autistic. She reads on a 1st/2nd grade level, can add, subtract up to digit numbers, tells time by a digital and analog clock— and because of that, this game is far too easy for her. She gets bored and starts answering the questions wrong just to see what will happen. Maybe update with a skills test, and then match the child up with curriculum that is in their skill range rather than age level"
    }
  ]
}
